d messages  then this is really handy also when i'm  like in a phone call on a zoom caller or  something then being able to silence  everything so that i am completely  focused on the task at hand  is something that has really helped me  to get my stuff done and right next to  do not disturb mode  is night shift now i used flux before  the invention of night shift on mac os  but it really does help me with my eyes  i have mine set to a schedule where i  have it turned  on at sunset and then turned on until  sunrise  i kind of like my scream to be a little  bit warmer like my eyes are already very  bad so they're doing a lot of work to  concentrate on a screen right in front  of me  and so taking away the blue light  definitely helps with my eyes so that i  can fall asleep at night  and i don't like blue light stuff  there's like a lot of competing debates  about it but really i have noticed that  since using night shift  i don't feel my eyeballs in my eyes when  i'm trying to fall asleep  and so i have night shift on my mac and  i also have it on my phone  and sometimes if my eyes are just tired  i'll just turn it on the middle of the  day like it really does just help  relieve my eye of that strain the next  thing i want to show you is  spotlight so unless you've changed the  settings on your own mac then you can  trigger spotlight with command  plus i have mine set so that'll search  through applications which really is  what i primarily use it for  and so instead of digging through like  the applications folder and trying to  find what you need  being able to go to spotlight search and  then type exactly what i need and then  press  enter and it launches thing makes it  super easy its origins are in something  called  alfred that a lot of people use at the  time that apple adopted and  it's made my workflow a lot easier and  it really helps you can have it set to  search through your different files as  well as different text  it can also even do math for you which  is really nice  uh and so that's just like a really  handy thing to have at your fingertips  another thing that i really like using  that's part of mac os is  spaces i have a lot of spaces all the  time and usually there's like an  application per space and i like this  because i like having full screen  windows for everything and it's easier  for me to navigate between different  spaces than it is  to navigate between different  applications so i usually have one for  video editing  another one for browsing the internet  another one for  text communication like discord and  whatsapp and i usually also have one for  spotify  i can always add more spaces i can also  take them away  and it's just been a good way to  organize all of my thoughts and the  applications that are running on my  computer  so those are the features that i enjoy  that are part of mac os  right now i'm using mac os catalina and  i'm really excited to use mac os big sur  as well as future releases to see what  functionality  apple adds to mac os while we're on the  topic of what's on my mac  i want to talk about one of my favorite  websites on my mac which is brilliant  who is a sponsor of today's video  brilliant is an amazing website and app  that makes learning super  accessible and fun their whole approach  is based on problem solving and  active learning which i think is really  kind of one of the best ways to learn  new things  they teach you concepts visually with  interactions to help you better  understand  and then also ask you questions so that  you're engaging with the topic  and content and unlike school there's  like no tests no grades  literally just to fulfill and enrich  your learning  they've got a ton of courses in math  science and computer  science one that i'm really interested  in that i'm going to take is the  knowledge and uncertainty course  this course gives you the mathematical  tools for dealing with the flood of  information in our lives  and you'll learn cutting edge  mathematics like information theory  bayesian networks and causal inference  but  in an accessible way without the  calculations getting in the way  they've also got a lot of great courses  in computer science as well  so whether you want to brush up on your  fundamentals and your algorithms or you  want to dive into something like python  or even cryptography  they've got it so try out brilliant  today by going to brilliant.org  hello myuco and only the first 200  subscribers will get 20  off the annual premium subscription  thanks so much for brilliant for  sponsoring and let's get back to the  video  so next we're going to talk about  applications so i have all of the  applications that i use on a day-to-day  basis  in my doc which shows and hides if i go  to the bottom of the screen  so we're going to start with brave brave  is a chromium-based browser that really  emphasizes privacy  it comes with built-in ad blockers and  everything and so it's been really  easy to use and i feel good using it  because it blocks all of these ads for  me to begin with  i actually used to use safari because  safari is also really good for privacy  but a lot of websites are built on top  of chromium and web developers are  mostly focused on delivering a good  experience on chrome and not so much  safari  which is why i've been using brave i  have my bookmarks at the top  and i have a couple of extensions but  that's  just that's kind of my web browser  there's not really a whole lot to it  there  so next let's talk about email i've been  using spark i use it on my phone and i  also  use the calendar for it as well i used  to just use the web browser for gmail  and then also the mail client on mac but  i needed something that was  a desktop app that also divided my email  just like how gmail does  i like that i can pin messages on spark  and that it divides my newsletters as  well as my notifications  and the calendar app's pretty intuitive  too there are some things that 